WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.160
You know, usually when we talk about a physical description of reality, there's this expectation of absolute precision, almost like engineering, really.

00:10.360 --> 00:12.380
Right. It's a very binary expectation.

00:12.540 --> 00:19.640
Yeah, exactly. Like you fall off a bike, you break your arm, you go to the hospital, and the x-ray shows that jagged white line on the bone.

00:20.260 --> 00:25.560
And the doctor just points at it and says, you know, there it is. That's the reality of your situation.

00:25.560 --> 00:29.980
It's very clean. It's broken or it's not broken. There's no in-between.

00:30.180 --> 00:40.880
Right. It's clean. And I think fundamentally, it's really comforting for us. I mean, we like things to be visible. We like them to be neatly categorized into little boxes.

00:40.960 --> 00:41.780
We definitely do.

00:41.780 --> 00:49.840
When we think about physics, especially the math we use to describe the universe, we like to think that the math is just, well, a transparent window.

00:50.120 --> 00:55.160
You look through the glass and there is the physics just sitting right there on the lawn exactly as it is.

00:55.160 --> 01:00.060
But, you know, that metaphor completely falls apart the moment you step into the world of quantum mechanics.

01:00.260 --> 01:00.560
Oh, entirely.

01:00.740 --> 01:09.140
Because suddenly that x-ray machine is just broken. We're no longer looking through a clear window. We're looking through, like, a kaleidoscope.

01:09.600 --> 01:11.160
Yeah, that's a great way to put it.

01:11.220 --> 01:16.760
For over a century now, physicists have used complex numbers to calculate how subatomic particles behave.

01:17.120 --> 01:23.380
And by complex numbers, I mean numbers with an imaginary component, you know, the square root of negative one.

01:23.380 --> 01:28.000
And just to be super clear for you listening, this isn't some fringe theory we're talking about today.

01:28.240 --> 01:28.820
No, not at all.

01:28.940 --> 01:30.380
This math works perfectly.

01:30.680 --> 01:30.840
Yeah.

01:30.840 --> 01:36.160
And it literally powers the semiconductors in the phone or the computer you're listening to this deep dive on right now.

01:36.400 --> 01:37.980
It is insanely accurate.

01:38.180 --> 01:41.220
It's arguably the most successful scientific theory we have.

01:41.320 --> 01:41.760
Exactly.

01:41.760 --> 01:49.320
But it leaves us with this massive, almost philosophical foundational question.

01:49.640 --> 01:51.940
Does the universe have to run on complex numbers?

01:52.180 --> 01:52.300
Right.

01:52.660 --> 01:59.780
Like, did reality order the complex number package or did we just wrap it in that box because, well, it made the math easier for us humans?

02:00.020 --> 02:03.860
It is the absolute definition of theoretical muddy waters.

02:04.180 --> 02:05.060
It really is.

02:05.060 --> 02:11.200
Are we discovering the universe's inherent operating system, like written in stone since the Big Bang?

02:11.680 --> 02:20.100
Or are we just inventing a really efficient programming language to describe what we see from our very limited human perspective?

02:20.500 --> 02:24.480
And that right there is our mission for this deep dive.

02:24.620 --> 02:24.760
Yeah.

02:24.960 --> 02:29.580
We are taking you on a journey to the very bedrock of physical reality today.

02:29.840 --> 02:32.940
We're basically going to try and reverse engineer quantum mechanics.

02:32.940 --> 02:33.380
Yes.

02:33.940 --> 02:42.400
I want you to imagine taking apart a beautiful, really intricate mechanical watch, but you're not doing it just to see how the little gears tick.

02:42.500 --> 02:42.700
Right.

02:42.760 --> 02:47.000
You're taking it apart to prove mathematically that it couldn't possibly have been built any other way.

02:47.200 --> 02:49.340
Or, you know, to prove that it actually could.

02:49.580 --> 02:52.320
Which brings us perfectly to the texts we are exploring today.

02:52.680 --> 02:59.200
I was recently reading through this massive stack of research originating from the Flyxion Research Group, published in 2026.

02:59.380 --> 03:00.400
And it's a lot, right?

03:00.400 --> 03:05.420
Oh, it's incredibly dense, but it completely challenged how I view mathematical physics.

03:05.660 --> 03:13.780
I have to say, when you sent these over to me, I was intimidated, to say the least, but also completely hooked once I got into it.

03:14.000 --> 03:15.340
It's a fascinating collection.

03:15.580 --> 03:19.820
First off, there's a companion piece in this stack called the RSVP Codex.

03:19.820 --> 03:28.820
And honestly, it reads less like a physics paper and more like a philosophical manifesto that got, like, hidden inside an abstract algebra textbook.

03:29.540 --> 03:30.980
That is a perfect description of it.

03:31.040 --> 03:37.800
It's basically an index of essays connecting these deep mathematical structures to human concepts.

03:37.800 --> 03:43.100
I mean, I'm talking about chapter titles like commutator ethics and the conservation of care.

03:43.680 --> 03:46.840
It sets this beautifully provocative tone right out of the gate.

03:47.140 --> 03:51.580
It suggests that the mathematical grammar we use isn't just cold, objective calculation.

03:52.120 --> 04:00.520
It implies that our math might actually reflect the specific way human observation and, well, human consciousness interface with reality.

04:00.520 --> 04:01.740
Which is a wild thought.

04:02.400 --> 04:11.620
But the main event for us today, the real heavy lifting of this deep dive, is a rigorous 78-page treatise titled Unistochastic Quantum Mechanics.

04:11.740 --> 04:13.420
And rigorous is really putting it mildly.

04:13.500 --> 04:14.660
It is brutally dense.

04:15.060 --> 04:19.520
But what makes this treatise so gripping, I think, is the author's method.

04:20.520 --> 04:26.400
You know how in a standard college physics class, the professor just writes the Schrodinger equation on the chalkboard

04:26.400 --> 04:31.260
and essentially says, accept this, memorize it, and let's move on.

04:31.320 --> 04:32.620
Just shut up and calculate, yeah?

04:32.620 --> 04:32.920
Exactly.

04:33.220 --> 04:34.440
The shut up and calculate approach.

04:34.880 --> 04:37.700
The author of this text absolutely refuses to do that.

04:37.780 --> 04:39.940
They refuse to just postulate quantum mechanics.

04:40.160 --> 04:43.880
They attempt to build the entire quantum universe from scratch.

04:43.880 --> 04:49.000
And they do it using nothing but basic rules about probability and information loss.

04:49.340 --> 04:54.260
What really stands out to me is the author's strict discipline here.

04:54.260 --> 04:56.860
They maintain what they call an assumption ledger.

04:57.120 --> 04:58.000
Yes, the ledger.

04:58.140 --> 04:59.040
I love this part.

04:59.120 --> 04:59.920
It's so cool.

05:00.240 --> 05:05.040
At the end of every single chapter, there is this brutal, uncompromising audit.

05:05.540 --> 05:10.320
A literal box on the page that states, here is exactly what we have mathematically proven,

05:10.580 --> 05:13.420
and here is exactly what we had to assume to get here.

05:13.700 --> 05:17.060
They don't let you sneak any hidden physics in through the back door.

05:17.560 --> 05:21.260
Every single piece of reality has to be meticulously accounted for.

05:21.620 --> 05:22.700
Okay, let's unpack this.

05:22.700 --> 05:28.700
Because if we're going to build the universe from scratch, we can't start with atoms or

05:28.700 --> 05:31.440
multidimensional space or entanglement.

05:31.560 --> 05:32.980
No, that's way too complex.

05:33.080 --> 05:35.700
We have to start at the absolute, bare bones bottom.

05:36.160 --> 05:38.620
We have to talk about basic states of knowledge.

05:39.180 --> 05:40.980
Essentially, we're talking about coin flips.

05:41.300 --> 05:41.600
Right.

05:41.960 --> 05:47.240
We begin with a geometric concept the text calls the probability simplex.

05:47.380 --> 05:49.000
Okay, that already sounds like heavy jargon.

05:49.400 --> 05:51.200
What exactly is a simplex?

05:51.200 --> 05:53.000
Don't let the name intimidate you.

05:53.160 --> 05:56.680
Think of it as a shape that just represents all possible states of knowledge.

05:56.840 --> 05:58.040
Okay, a shape of knowledge.

05:58.120 --> 05:58.340
Yeah.

05:58.560 --> 06:03.420
Let's say you have a system with a set of distinct, distinguishable outcomes.

06:03.560 --> 06:07.820
It could be the six faces of a die or, you know, different clicks on a Geiger counter.

06:08.000 --> 06:08.200
Okay.

06:08.440 --> 06:08.900
Simple of?

06:09.200 --> 06:12.920
A state of knowledge is just a list of probabilities for those outcomes.

06:12.920 --> 06:18.700
So, if I have a loaded die, my list might say there's a 50% chance of rolling a six and maybe

06:18.700 --> 06:21.160
a 10% chance for each of the other five numbers.

06:21.840 --> 06:22.240
Exactly.

06:22.540 --> 06:25.760
And all those percentages, they have to add up to 100%.

06:25.760 --> 06:28.420
You are 100% sure something is going to happen.

06:28.720 --> 06:30.260
You just don't know exactly what.

06:30.260 --> 06:31.920
That's the fundamental rule of probability.

06:32.100 --> 06:32.260
Right.

06:32.540 --> 06:36.580
So, the probabilities cannot be negative and they must sum to one.

06:37.220 --> 06:43.540
Now, geometrically, if you graph all those possible valid lists of probabilities, they

06:43.540 --> 06:47.340
form a shape and that shape is the probability simplex.

06:47.420 --> 06:47.560
Right.

06:47.560 --> 06:51.520
The sharp corners of this shape represent absolute certainty.

06:52.000 --> 06:55.920
For example, a state where you are 100% sure you will roll a six.

06:56.400 --> 06:57.100
That's a corner.

06:57.420 --> 07:01.900
And then everything inside the volume of the shape, that represents some degree of uncertainty,

07:02.100 --> 07:02.260
right?

07:02.360 --> 07:03.400
Like my loaded die.

07:04.000 --> 07:04.520
Precisely.

07:04.920 --> 07:10.020
Now, if we want to build a physical theory, we need to mathematically describe how that system

07:10.020 --> 07:10.940
changes over time.

07:11.080 --> 07:15.020
So, we use a transition matrix, specifically something called a stochastic map.

07:15.160 --> 07:16.400
Well, let me make sure I'm following this.

07:16.400 --> 07:22.040
A stochastic map, if I have my list of probabilities today, my state of knowledge right now, this

07:22.040 --> 07:25.700
map is just the mathematical rule that tells me what my probabilities will be tomorrow.

07:26.060 --> 07:27.480
That's a perfect way to look at it.

07:27.620 --> 07:32.380
And the defining rule for a stochastic matrix is that its columns have to add up to one.

07:33.000 --> 07:35.280
Because probability can't just appear or disappear.

07:35.580 --> 07:36.020
Exactly.

07:36.620 --> 07:40.300
Probability cannot be created out of thin air and it cannot be magically destroyed.

07:40.760 --> 07:45.640
If you start with a valid state of knowledge, the map guarantees you end up with a valid

07:45.640 --> 07:46.500
state of knowledge.

07:46.600 --> 07:47.100
That makes sense.

07:47.160 --> 07:48.360
It's just strict accounting.

07:48.980 --> 07:54.400
But here is where the author adds the very first physical constraint to our whole universe

07:54.400 --> 07:55.060
building project.

07:55.600 --> 07:58.920
The text introduces by stochastic maps.

07:59.260 --> 07:59.960
By stochastic.

08:00.080 --> 08:01.440
So, the prefix by meaning two.

08:01.680 --> 08:01.940
Right.

08:02.200 --> 08:06.180
Not only do the columns have to sum to one, but the rows have to sum to one as well.

08:06.240 --> 08:06.420
Okay.

08:06.420 --> 08:07.660
I can see the symmetry there.

08:07.740 --> 08:08.720
It's mathematically neat.

08:09.240 --> 08:10.480
But why does that matter physically?

08:10.840 --> 08:13.000
Why do we care about the rows adding up to one?

08:13.220 --> 08:18.840
Because row normalization introduces this vital concept of reversibility and information

08:18.840 --> 08:19.620
preservation.

08:19.980 --> 08:20.420
Reversibility.

08:20.520 --> 08:20.680
Okay.

08:21.240 --> 08:23.420
Let's think about a deterministic map.

08:23.800 --> 08:27.660
A rule that simply relabels outcomes without losing any underlying detail.

08:28.240 --> 08:32.760
The text highlights a foundational piece of math here called the Berkhoff von Neumann theorem.

08:32.760 --> 08:34.380
Oh, that sounds serious.

08:34.540 --> 08:34.980
It is.

08:35.220 --> 08:37.240
But it proves something quite beautiful.

08:37.880 --> 08:44.180
It proves that this entire geometric space of bistochastic maps, a shape known as the Berkhoff

08:44.180 --> 08:48.440
polytope, is completely defined by perfect permutations.

08:48.800 --> 08:49.360
Permutations.

08:49.560 --> 08:51.780
Like shuffling a deck of cards.

08:51.940 --> 08:52.840
Yes, exactly.

08:53.300 --> 08:55.340
Think about what happens when you shuffle a deck.

08:55.800 --> 08:57.260
You aren't destroying any cards.

08:57.360 --> 08:59.740
You aren't cutting two cards in half and gluing them together.

08:59.840 --> 09:00.040
Right.

09:00.100 --> 09:01.800
Just perfectly swapping their positions.

09:01.800 --> 09:03.440
It's entirely reversible.

09:03.660 --> 09:05.740
If I recorded the shuffle, I could just unshuffle it.

09:06.000 --> 09:06.400
Precisely.

09:06.720 --> 09:11.000
The extreme points, the sharpest corners of this Berkhoff polytope shape we're talking

09:11.000 --> 09:15.520
about, are exactly these perfect reversible shuffles.

09:15.580 --> 09:15.820
Okay.

09:15.980 --> 09:16.460
I'm with you.

09:16.660 --> 09:22.280
And any point inside the shape, any bistochastic map at all, can be mathematically expressed

09:22.280 --> 09:24.540
as a mixture of these perfect shuffles.

09:24.780 --> 09:28.320
Let me try an analogy here to see if I'm grasping the physical consequence of this.

09:28.320 --> 09:32.520
It sounds like the difference between mixing paint and just swapping sealed cans of paint.

09:32.640 --> 09:33.580
I like where this is going.

09:33.580 --> 09:38.500
So let's say I have a sealed can of red paint and a sealed can of blue paint on a shelf.

09:39.000 --> 09:41.520
If I swap their places, I haven't lost any information.

09:41.780 --> 09:42.520
That's a permutation.

09:42.780 --> 09:42.860
Right.

09:43.020 --> 09:44.400
I can easily swap them back.

09:44.460 --> 09:46.860
I know exactly where the red is and exactly where the blue is.

09:46.860 --> 09:53.980
But if I open the cans, pour the red paint and the blue paint into a single bucket and stir

09:53.980 --> 09:56.140
them together, I've created purple.

09:56.920 --> 09:58.600
And can you unmix that bucket?

09:58.880 --> 09:59.280
No.

09:59.880 --> 10:04.380
I can never, ever pull the pure red and pure blue back out of that purple bucket.

10:04.760 --> 10:06.300
I've coarse grained the system.

10:06.640 --> 10:09.480
I've permanently lost the original distinct information.

10:09.760 --> 10:11.300
That is a brilliant analogy.

10:11.780 --> 10:15.500
Mixing the paint is what happens when you blend those perfect permutations together.

10:15.500 --> 10:19.760
And this leads directly to a profound physical consequence involving entropy.

10:20.340 --> 10:20.500
Entropy.

10:20.780 --> 10:21.920
The measurement of disorder.

10:22.080 --> 10:22.440
Exactly.

10:22.820 --> 10:25.660
The text leans on a mathematical concept called majorization.

10:26.040 --> 10:29.840
I won't drag us through the dense equations, but essentially, majorization proves that if

10:29.840 --> 10:35.060
you evolve a system using a bistochastic map, if you mix those paint cans, you can never,

10:35.380 --> 10:37.900
ever decrease the Shannon entropy of your knowledge.

10:38.120 --> 10:38.360
Okay.

10:38.520 --> 10:40.420
Let's pause on Shannon entropy for a second.

10:40.420 --> 10:45.180
For someone listening who hasn't taken an information theory class, what does that actually

10:45.180 --> 10:46.960
mean in the context of our paint bucket?

10:47.520 --> 10:51.480
Shannon entropy is essentially a measure of your surprise or your uncertainty.

10:51.820 --> 10:51.960
Okay.

10:52.040 --> 10:57.160
If you know for a fact the can contains pure red paint, your uncertainty is zero.

10:57.440 --> 10:58.960
You aren't surprised when you open it.

10:59.060 --> 11:00.200
The entropy is zero.

11:00.260 --> 11:00.420
Right.

11:00.480 --> 11:02.080
Because I have perfect knowledge.

11:02.080 --> 11:07.780
But if you pour red, blue, and yellow into a bucket and stir, and I ask you to pull out

11:07.780 --> 11:13.180
a single drop and tell me what color the original pigments were, your uncertainty is massive.

11:13.540 --> 11:13.760
Yeah.

11:13.840 --> 11:14.580
I have no idea.

11:14.820 --> 11:14.980
Right.

11:15.060 --> 11:16.380
So the entropy has increased.

11:16.900 --> 11:22.000
The math of majorization proves that under bistochastic evolution, your uncertainty can

11:22.000 --> 11:23.860
only stay the same or go up.

11:24.000 --> 11:26.020
Meaning things can only get more purple.

11:26.020 --> 11:32.120
The universe can never spontaneously unmix the bucket back into perfect red and perfect blue.

11:32.200 --> 11:32.600
Exactly.

11:32.960 --> 11:35.360
And let's consider the broader implications of that.

11:35.540 --> 11:37.120
Notice what the author didn't do here.

11:37.200 --> 11:37.780
What didn't they do?

11:37.940 --> 11:41.060
They didn't import the second law of thermodynamics.

11:41.400 --> 11:47.000
They didn't borrow concepts from like 19th century steam engine physics to force the universe

11:47.000 --> 11:48.240
to behave a certain way.

11:48.360 --> 11:48.740
Oh, why?

11:48.740 --> 11:54.740
They proved that entropy must increase as a purely structural geometric consequence of the

11:54.740 --> 11:55.720
Burkhoff polytope.

11:56.580 --> 11:59.400
The geometry forces the physics to exist.

11:59.760 --> 12:00.900
That is incredibly cool.

12:01.040 --> 12:06.480
We've derived the arrow of time, essentially, just from drawing a shape with rows and columns

12:06.480 --> 12:07.320
that add up to one.

12:07.440 --> 12:08.740
It's elegant, isn't it?

12:08.820 --> 12:09.460
It really is.

12:09.800 --> 12:11.820
But I have to push back here a little bit.

12:11.900 --> 12:15.300
This all sounds like classical high school level probability.

12:15.460 --> 12:16.320
We're shuffling cards.

12:16.420 --> 12:17.120
We're mixing paint.

12:17.200 --> 12:17.440
Sure.

12:17.540 --> 12:18.480
When does it get weird?

12:19.300 --> 12:22.880
The whole premise of this deep dive was about quantum mechanics and complex numbers.

12:22.880 --> 12:28.280
Why can't we just build the universe out of these regular classical real number of probabilities?

12:28.680 --> 12:30.700
The author asks that exact same question.

12:31.160 --> 12:35.920
And to answer it, we have to look at how we lift these bare classical probabilities into

12:35.920 --> 12:37.120
something more fundamental.

12:37.520 --> 12:37.800
Lift them.

12:37.960 --> 12:38.320
How do you mean?

12:38.840 --> 12:42.200
Well, in quantum mechanics, probabilities don't just exist on their own.

12:42.460 --> 12:44.820
They are the squares of underlying amplitudes.

12:45.180 --> 12:46.220
Ah, amplitudes.

12:46.220 --> 12:46.780
Right.

12:47.200 --> 12:52.360
The text defines a transition matrix built out of these amplitudes as a unistochastic

12:52.360 --> 12:55.240
matrix, which is basically built from a unitary matrix.

12:55.640 --> 13:00.340
And unitary matrices are where complex numbers, the imaginary eye, where they live.

13:00.420 --> 13:00.600
Okay.

13:00.700 --> 13:05.480
So a unistochastic matrix relies on the complex math we currently use in standard physics.

13:05.700 --> 13:06.020
Yes.

13:06.660 --> 13:10.820
The alternative, if we want to completely banish imaginary numbers and just use real numbers,

13:10.820 --> 13:13.220
is to build an orthostochastic matrix.

13:13.620 --> 13:14.180
Orthostochastic.

13:14.180 --> 13:16.840
Which is built purely out of real numbers, real amplitudes.

13:17.160 --> 13:21.480
So the entire fate of the universe basically boils down to this one question.

13:22.040 --> 13:27.080
Can every single unistochastic matrix be flawlessly reproduced by an orthostochastic one?

13:27.240 --> 13:30.940
Meaning, can real numbers do absolutely everything complex numbers can do?

13:31.200 --> 13:31.720
Exactly.

13:32.120 --> 13:35.820
Well, based on the fact that we use complex numbers to run our computers, I'm going to

13:35.820 --> 13:37.320
guess the answer is a hard no.

13:37.320 --> 13:42.540
It's actually a bit of a mathematical trap, because if your system only has two possible

13:42.540 --> 13:46.100
outcomes, what physicists call n equals two, the answer is yes.

13:46.220 --> 13:46.860
Wait, really?

13:46.920 --> 13:47.140
Yeah.

13:47.400 --> 13:50.480
At n equals two, the math shows a perfect degeneracy.

13:50.960 --> 13:52.740
Real numbers work flawlessly.

13:53.240 --> 13:54.660
So just a simple coin flip.

13:55.280 --> 13:55.900
Heads or tails?

13:56.140 --> 13:56.400
Right.

13:56.680 --> 14:02.340
If you observe a single two-outcome system, a single coin flip, you cannot tell whether the

14:02.340 --> 14:05.720
underlying reality is running on real numbers or complex numbers.

14:05.720 --> 14:08.400
The math overlaps perfectly.

14:08.700 --> 14:09.040
Oh, wow.

14:09.160 --> 14:15.140
So if the universe only consisted of simple yes or no binary particles, real numbers would

14:15.140 --> 14:16.120
be totally sufficient.

14:16.260 --> 14:17.920
We wouldn't need imaginary math at all.

14:18.040 --> 14:18.520
That's right.

14:19.060 --> 14:24.500
But the moment you add a third outcome at n equals three, like a three-sided die or a

14:24.500 --> 14:27.600
particle that can spin up, down, or sideways, everything breaks.

14:27.700 --> 14:28.640
Everything breaks at three.

14:28.720 --> 14:29.340
How does it break?

14:29.460 --> 14:30.000
Walk me through it.

14:30.120 --> 14:33.180
The author provides a devastatingly simple counterexample.

14:33.180 --> 14:37.260
It's called the discrete Fourier transform matrix, or DFT.

14:37.620 --> 14:38.680
Okay, a Fourier transform.

14:38.820 --> 14:40.320
I know that's used in audio engineering, right?

14:40.400 --> 14:42.840
Like to break down sound waves into different frequencies.

14:43.000 --> 14:43.140
Yes.

14:43.220 --> 14:46.360
It's a very standard, highly symmetric mathematical operation.

14:46.720 --> 14:50.780
And in quantum mechanics, a three-by-three DFT matrix is used all the time.

14:51.140 --> 14:53.580
The probabilities it generates are totally uniform.

14:53.740 --> 14:54.600
They're perfectly flat.

14:55.080 --> 15:00.600
But if you try to build that exact same uniform probability transition using only real numbers,

15:00.780 --> 15:02.720
using an orthostochastic matrix.

15:02.920 --> 15:03.020
Yeah.

15:03.180 --> 15:07.840
The math demands that you find a three-by-three matrix made entirely of positive and negative

15:07.840 --> 15:11.040
ones, whose rows are all perfectly perpendicular to each other.

15:11.140 --> 15:12.380
Wait, I'm trying to visualize this.

15:12.660 --> 15:13.780
A three-by-three grid.

15:14.460 --> 15:15.660
Nine boxes total.

15:16.040 --> 15:19.920
And the only numbers I'm allowed to put in those boxes are a positive one or a negative

15:19.920 --> 15:20.220
one.

15:20.300 --> 15:20.580
Yes.

15:20.580 --> 15:26.500
And every row must be mathematically orthogonal, meaning perpendicular, to every other row.

15:26.640 --> 15:27.040
Exactly.

15:27.040 --> 15:31.400
This specific structure is known in mathematics as a Hadamard matrix.

