Yes.

37:14.620 --> 37:19.420
The entanglement is an illusion caused entirely by the English speaker's limited grammar.

37:19.760 --> 37:22.400
It's totally separable once you have the right vocabulary.

37:22.400 --> 37:27.120
It forces us to take a massive step back and question absolutely everything.

37:27.480 --> 37:32.640
How much of quantum weirdness is a profound, inescapable truth about reality?

37:33.000 --> 37:37.520
And how much of it is just a shadow cast by our choice of mathematical notation?

37:37.920 --> 37:39.900
Which brings us to the assumption audit.

37:40.400 --> 37:45.600
Because if quantum mechanics is so heavily dependent on the mathematical grammar we choose

37:45.600 --> 37:50.840
to use, what do we actually have to assume to build the standard, complex quantum theory

37:50.840 --> 37:51.640
we use today?

37:51.640 --> 37:58.260
After 78 pages of uncompromising dense derivation, the author tallies up the final bill.

37:58.900 --> 38:05.180
Out of all the possible ways to build a universe, the author identifies exactly four independent

38:05.180 --> 38:08.700
physical assumptions used to recover standard quantum mechanics.

38:08.840 --> 38:09.140
Four.

38:09.460 --> 38:12.260
Just four pillars holding up the entire sky.

38:12.380 --> 38:12.920
Only four.

38:13.020 --> 38:14.540
Let's walk through them one by one.

38:14.820 --> 38:15.300
Number one.

38:15.820 --> 38:16.960
Local tomography.

38:16.960 --> 38:22.440
The assumption we just spent all that time discussing, the idea that joint states are

38:22.440 --> 38:24.540
fully described by local measurements.

38:24.680 --> 38:24.900
Right.

38:25.100 --> 38:27.100
Remember, the author didn't derive this as a given.

38:27.520 --> 38:32.100
They assumed it, tested it, and found that complex numbers pass the test perfectly.

38:32.240 --> 38:32.400
Right.

38:32.500 --> 38:35.040
The 16 equals 16 dimension match we did earlier.

38:35.200 --> 38:35.940
That's pillar one.

38:36.040 --> 38:36.760
What is number two?

38:36.940 --> 38:37.280
Two.

38:37.900 --> 38:40.040
Pure states are frame-completable vectors.

38:40.280 --> 38:41.220
Frame-completable vectors.

38:41.300 --> 38:42.700
Let's translate that for everyone.

38:42.700 --> 38:47.420
In simple terms, it means that if you have a state of absolute certainty, a pure state,

38:47.540 --> 38:51.960
where you know exactly what the system is doing, you can mathematically treat that state

38:51.960 --> 38:54.880
as just one column in a larger transition matrix.

38:54.980 --> 38:56.620
You can complete the frame around it.

38:56.980 --> 38:57.420
Exactly.

38:57.780 --> 39:00.600
You complete the frame to map out all the other possibilities.

39:00.800 --> 39:02.860
And what does assuming that actually buy us?

39:03.000 --> 39:03.940
It buys us everything.

39:04.520 --> 39:09.000
By making this one geometric assumption, the author instantly derives the Born Rule.

39:09.000 --> 39:14.920
The Born Rule is the fundamental law that tells us how to calculate the probability of a quantum event happening.

39:15.060 --> 39:15.280
Right.

39:15.560 --> 39:16.720
You square the amplitude.

39:17.100 --> 39:17.480
Exactly.

39:17.960 --> 39:21.400
In most textbooks, the Born Rule is just handed to you as a postulate.

39:21.800 --> 39:23.000
This is how probability works.

39:23.180 --> 39:23.860
Don't ask why.

39:24.040 --> 39:24.820
Just accept it.

39:24.920 --> 39:25.100
Right.

39:25.580 --> 39:33.280
But here, the Born Rule emerges organically just from assuming that pure states can be placed inside a complete mathematical frame.

39:33.440 --> 39:34.500
That's incredibly elegant.

39:35.160 --> 39:35.360
Okay.

39:35.560 --> 39:36.340
Pillar number three.

39:36.340 --> 39:36.900
Three.

39:36.900 --> 39:40.540
Measurement outcomes are labeled by real numbers.

39:41.000 --> 39:41.020
Okay.

39:41.100 --> 39:42.280
That feels incredibly obvious.

39:42.760 --> 39:46.440
If I measure the weight of a quantum particle, my digital scale gives me a real number.

39:46.560 --> 39:47.760
It says 5.2.

39:47.980 --> 39:49.600
It doesn't give me an imaginary number.

39:49.720 --> 39:54.960
It seems obvious to our human experience, but mathematically, this is a profound choice.

39:55.220 --> 39:55.600
How so?

39:55.800 --> 40:01.600
This specific assumption is what forces quantum observables to be described by Hermitian matrices.

40:01.600 --> 40:10.120
It forces the math to ensure that the eigenvalues, the actual readings on your digital scale, are strictly real numbers.

40:10.520 --> 40:10.880
Ah.

40:11.120 --> 40:11.220
Okay.

40:11.220 --> 40:14.620
It's a choice about how we label the reality we interact with.

40:14.740 --> 40:16.660
And finally, pillar number four.

40:17.060 --> 40:17.380
Four.

40:17.960 --> 40:21.740
Time evolution is a strongly continuous unitary group.

40:21.740 --> 40:24.100
Let me focus on the word continuous there.

40:24.780 --> 40:26.180
Time flows smoothly.

40:27.100 --> 40:32.240
The universe doesn't tick forward in discrete, chunky frames like a 60 frames per second video game.

40:32.360 --> 40:32.620
Yes.

40:32.900 --> 40:34.440
Time is smooth and reversible.

40:34.900 --> 40:41.280
And from that assumption of smooth, continuous evolution, the author derives the Schrodinger equation.

40:41.440 --> 40:42.000
Just from that.

40:42.000 --> 40:45.840
And they do it just from a simple, finite-dimensional, ordinary differential equation.

40:45.940 --> 40:50.760
They don't even need the heavy, infinite-dimensional math usually required in textbooks, like Stone's theorem.

40:51.000 --> 40:51.700
I have to pause here.

40:51.900 --> 40:52.540
That's really it.

40:52.560 --> 40:53.300
Just four rules.

40:53.420 --> 40:53.760
That's it.

40:53.980 --> 40:55.300
You assume local tomography.

40:55.940 --> 40:58.280
You assume states can be completed into frames.

40:58.580 --> 41:00.460
You use real numbers for your dials.

41:00.780 --> 41:02.460
And you assume time flows smoothly.

41:03.020 --> 41:08.820
And from those four seeds, you grow the entire massive, complex tree of quantum mechanics.

41:08.900 --> 41:09.320
You do.

41:09.480 --> 41:10.480
It scales perfectly.

41:10.480 --> 41:20.300
Even highly complex fuzzy measurements, known in physics as POVMs or positive operator-valued measures, emerge naturally.

41:20.500 --> 41:22.060
How do fuzzy measurements fit in?

41:22.260 --> 41:25.520
The text uses a mathematical trick called the Neumark dilation.

41:25.640 --> 41:27.280
What does a Neumark dilation do?

41:27.540 --> 41:32.620
Imagine you are trying to measure a quantum system, but your ruler is blurry.

41:32.960 --> 41:34.480
It's a fuzzy, generalized measurement.

41:35.240 --> 41:36.420
Okay, I've got a blurry ruler.

41:36.420 --> 41:43.500
The Neumark dilation proves that any fuzzy measurement is actually just a perfectly sharp, clean measurement happening on a larger system.

41:43.620 --> 41:44.420
Oh, that's wild.

41:44.560 --> 41:49.620
You just couple your little system to a larger environment, evolve them smoothly together, and then measure the whole thing sharply.

41:49.920 --> 41:53.540
Everything we observe in quantum labs scales perfectly from those four original seeds.

41:53.540 --> 42:03.320
On one hand, four is an astonishingly small number of assumptions to generate the most successful, most heavily tested physical theory in human history.

42:03.560 --> 42:04.440
It really is.

42:04.440 --> 42:08.780
But, on the other hand, it proves that quantum mechanics isn't inevitable.

42:09.260 --> 42:11.560
It's not the only way the universe could have been programmed.

42:12.180 --> 42:13.560
It is a specific route.

42:14.040 --> 42:17.820
We chose those four assumptions based on how we interact with the world.

42:18.080 --> 42:21.880
Which sets us up perfectly for the massive plot twist in the final chapters.

42:22.000 --> 42:23.140
Yes, the plot twist.

42:23.140 --> 42:33.980
Just when the book has thoroughly convinced us that complex numbers are absolutely mandatory because they are the only field that passes the local tomography dimension counting test perfectly.

42:34.160 --> 42:35.960
The author pulls the rug out from under us.

42:36.020 --> 42:37.000
A total rug pull.

42:37.480 --> 42:44.120
Because they cite groundbreaking new research published right around the same time in 2025 and 2026.

42:44.520 --> 42:45.640
Let's talk about those papers.

42:45.880 --> 42:51.100
There are papers by Haforman and Woods and another major paper by Barrios Hita and colleagues.

42:51.100 --> 42:55.520
These papers attack the very foundation of the local tomography dimension counting.

42:56.040 --> 42:56.060
Okay.

42:56.220 --> 42:59.160
They target the mathematical grammar we use to count the parameters.

42:59.480 --> 43:01.240
They target the Kronecker product.

43:01.560 --> 43:02.500
The Kronecker product.

43:02.820 --> 43:03.080
Okay.

43:03.260 --> 43:03.760
Let me review.

43:04.380 --> 43:13.460
When we were counting dimensions earlier, when we said four parameters times four parameters equals 16 for complex numbers, or three times three equals nine for real numbers,

43:13.700 --> 43:20.200
we assumed a specific mathematical rule for how you multiply those matrices together to combine the systems.

43:20.200 --> 43:20.620
Yes.

43:20.620 --> 43:22.900
That rule is the Kronecker product.

43:23.240 --> 43:25.180
We assumed it because it is standard.

43:25.420 --> 43:27.660
It is the default grammar of quantum mechanics.

43:27.940 --> 43:29.060
But it is an assumption.

43:29.380 --> 43:30.500
It's a choice of representation.

43:30.940 --> 43:31.220
Okay.

43:31.500 --> 43:36.280
What these 2025 and 2026 papers show is that if you change the composition rule,

43:37.100 --> 43:40.740
you change the mathematical grammar of how two systems combine on paper,

43:41.520 --> 43:44.640
you can completely circumvent the failure of real numbers.

43:44.860 --> 43:46.460
Let me make sure I'm wrapping my head around this.

43:46.540 --> 43:47.740
You don't change the physics.

43:47.920 --> 43:49.140
You don't change the particles.

43:49.140 --> 43:49.460
No.

43:49.460 --> 43:53.180
You just change the arithmetic rule for how you multiply the matrices on your chalkboard.

43:53.260 --> 43:53.700
Exactly.

43:53.700 --> 43:58.260
Specifically, they introduce a different tensor product, denoted with a little r,

43:58.260 --> 44:01.260
that mathematically quotients out what's known as phase redistribution.

44:02.060 --> 44:03.420
Let's break down phase redistribution.

44:03.820 --> 44:09.960
In complex quantum mechanics, a global phase, a sort of overall mathematical shift in the wave,

44:10.100 --> 44:16.600
can be arbitrarily assigned to particle A or particle B without changing any of the observable physics.

44:16.600 --> 44:17.040
Right.

44:17.160 --> 44:17.780
It's ambiguous.

44:18.060 --> 44:23.640
The standard Kronecker product, when forced to use real numbers, gets horribly tangled up by this ambiguity.

44:23.980 --> 44:27.200
It creates phantom mathematical dimensions to account for it,

44:27.240 --> 44:31.040
which is exactly why we ended up with 10 dimensions instead of 9 earlier.

44:31.320 --> 44:34.380
The grammar was creating dimensions that didn't physically exist.

44:34.380 --> 44:35.980
It's like the new grammar fixes it.

44:35.980 --> 44:41.920
If you quotient out that ambiguity mathematically, if you tell the math to ignore that phase shift,

44:42.120 --> 44:45.680
you can reproduce all of quantum mechanics using only real numbers.

44:45.840 --> 44:46.140
All of it.

44:46.400 --> 44:53.120
Every single empirical prediction, every experiment, replicated perfectly without a single imaginary eye.

44:53.120 --> 44:57.480
So wait, we can build the universe with real numbers after all.

44:57.640 --> 44:58.280
We can.

44:58.740 --> 45:01.540
If that's true, then why on earth do we use complex numbers?

45:01.980 --> 45:05.360
Where does the imaginary eye come from if reality doesn't actually need it?

45:05.500 --> 45:07.500
This is perhaps my favorite part of the entire text.

45:07.600 --> 45:09.040
This is where we talk about the amplitwist.

45:09.340 --> 45:10.460
The amplitwist.

45:10.900 --> 45:13.160
That sounds like a 1960s dance move.

45:13.360 --> 45:18.380
It does, but the author cites Tristan Needham's work on visual complex analysis.

45:19.260 --> 45:20.760
What is a complex number, really?

45:20.760 --> 45:22.900
We get so hung up on the word imaginary.

45:23.220 --> 45:24.020
